provided that all requirements of this License are followed.

No fee is required to exercise these rights.

## 3. Free Use Requirement

SmaulBRAIN and Covered Works must be freely available to their users unless the copyright holder has granted explicit Permission for Commercial Use.

A person or organization may use SmaulBRAIN for free for personal, educational, scientific, research, nonprofit, hobbyist, or internal organizational purposes.

Organizations may also use SmaulBRAIN internally for commercial purposes without charging users for SmaulBRAIN itself, provided that such use does not violate the other requirements of this License.

The default rule is:

**SmaulBRAIN-based software and services must be provided free of charge.**

## 4. Prohibition on Commercial Distribution

Without explicit Permission, no person or organization may engage in Commercial Use of a Covered Work.

Without limiting the meaning of Commercial Use, the following are prohibited without Permission:

1. selling SmaulBRAIN or a Covered Work;
2. selling access to SmaulBRAIN;
3. charging for API tokens or API requests generated by a Covered Work;
4. charging for inference credits;
5. charging subscriptions for access to a Covered Work;
6. charging users for hosted access to a Covered Work;
7. selling a SmaulBRAIN-based SaaS product;
8. selling a SmaulBRAIN-based inference service;
9. selling access to a model substantially implemented using SmaulBRAIN;
10. licensing a Covered Work as proprietary software;
11. placing a Covered Work behind a paywall;
12. charging customers for functionality whose substantial implementation is provided by a Covered Work;
13. bundling a Covered Work into a paid product where the Covered Work is a substantial component of the product;
14. using a Covered Work to provide a paid service to third parties.

These restrictions apply regardless of whether payment is made directly, indirectly, through subscriptions, credits, tokens, advertising-supported access, usage-based billing, licensing fees, donations required for access, or another substantially equivalent mechanism.

## 5. Cost-Based Commercial Exceptions

The copyright holder recognizes that operating large models and hosted computing infrastructure can sometimes involve substantial unavoidable costs.

If a person or organization reasonably believes that providing a SmaulBRAIN-based service free of charge is impractical because of substantial operating, infrastructure, hardware, storage, bandwidth, or other unavoidable costs, that person or organization may request Permission to charge users.

A request may be made through:

* a GitHub issue;
* a GitHub discussion;
* an email to the copyright holder; or
* another communication channel explicitly designated by the copyright holder.

The request should explain:

* what is being built;
* how SmaulBRAIN is being used;
* why the service cannot reasonably be provided free of charge;
* what users will be charged;
* how the charges relate to the actual costs of providing the service.

Commercial Use is **not permitted merely because a Permission request has been submitted**.

Commercial Use may begin only after explicit written Permission has been granted.

Permission may be granted with additional conditions, limitations, duration, pricing requirements, attribution requirements, or other terms.

Permission granted to one person or organization does not automatically grant Permission to another person or organization.

## 6. Source Availability

Any person distributing a Covered Work must make the corresponding source code available to recipients under this License.

The source must be provided in a form that allows a reasonable person to:

* inspect the implementation;
* modify the implementation;
* build the software;
* reproduce the distributed version to a reasonable extent.

A distributor may not distribute a Covered Work in a form that intentionally prevents recipients from accessing the corresponding source code.

A Covered Work may not be converted into proprietary closed-source software while retaining SmaulBRAIN code or substantial copyrighted portions of SmaulBRAIN.

## 7. Same-License Requirement

Derivative Works containing or substantially derived from SmaulBRAIN source code must be distributed under this License unless the copyright holder grants written Permission for another licensing arrangement.

This requirement applies regardless of whether the modified code:

* is moved to different files;
* is renamed;
* is translated into another programming language;
* is reorganized;
* is split into multiple packages;
* is combined with other software;
* is substantially modified.

Changing names, file locations, programming languages, interfaces, or organization does not by itself remove obligations under this License.

## 8. Hosted Services and Network Use

If a Covered Work is used to operate a Service, the operator must provide the corresponding source code of the Covered Work to users in a reasonably accessible manner.

The operator must not use a network interface, API, hosted environment, container, remote execution system, or similar mechanism as a means of avoiding the source-availability or free-use requirements of this License.

Simply running SmaulBRAIN on a remote server does not transform the Covered Work into proprietary software.

A hosted SmaulBRAIN-based Service remains subject to this License.

## 9. Attribution

Every redistribution of SmaulBRAIN or a Covered Work must include clear attribution to the original project.

The attribution must identify:

**SmaulBRAIN — Byte-level Recurrent Adaptive Intelligent Network**

and must identify the original SmaulBRAIN repository.

The attribution must be reasonably visible in the documentation, credits, about page, repository, or other appropriate location.

A distributor may add its own attribution and copyright notices, but may not remove or obscure the original attribution.

The distributor must not represent SmaulBRAIN or a Covered Work as having been originally created by the distributor.

